.TH STARTS 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
starts \- list the function entry addresses a binary records
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B starts
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RB [ \-\-symbols ]
.RB [ \-\-max= n ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Lists every function entry the linker recorded, read from
.BR LC_FUNCTION_STARTS .
.PP
That command is the linker's own list of where functions begin, and it is the
.I only
such list a stripped binary carries: the symbol table is gone, but the command
survives because the unwinder needs it at runtime. On a shipped build it is the
difference between a column of addresses and no structure at all; on a symballed
one it is a cross\-check on the symbol values.
.PP
Each address is labeled
.BR sub_<hex> .
That is a name for the address, not a claim about what the function does, but it
is what turns a list of numbers into something a person can scan and a pipeline
can key on.
.SH PRESENT, EMPTY, AND ABSENT
Three outcomes, kept apart because they are three different facts:
.TP
.B present
the file carries an
.B LC_FUNCTION_STARTS
blob, and
.B count
says how many entries it decoded.
.TP
.B count 0
the blob is present but empty: the linker recorded no functions.
.TP
.B present false
the file carries no
.B LC_FUNCTION_STARTS
at all. An object file and a hand\-built binary usually have none.
.PP
The last two exit 1, because the tool answered the question and the answer was
no. A caller that reads "nothing" as a crash will not look again.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-symbols
Add the defined symbol sitting on each start, when there is one. A start with no
symbol on it keeps its
.B sub_<hex>
label.
.TP
.BI \-\-max= n
Cap the list. Default 0, meaning all. The
.B count
field stays exact, so a capped list is distinguishable from a short one.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Read one architecture by name:
.BR x86_64 ,
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e ,
.BR arm64_32 ,
.BR ppc ,
.BR ppc64 ,
.BR arm
or
.BR i386 .
A universal binary maps
.B __TEXT
at 0x100000000 in
.I every
slice, so without this flag the first slice is read.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read. May also be given as the first positional argument.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings: a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive a JSON number.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_EXPLORER_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_EXPLORER_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The linker recorded function starts.
.TP
.B 1
There are none: the blob is empty, or the file carries no
.B LC_FUNCTION_STARTS
at all. A negative result, not a failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including a
.B \-\-max
that is not a whole number >= 0.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read or parsed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
.nf
starts /usr/local/go/bin/go
starts \-\-symbols \-\-max=20 /usr/local/go/bin/go
starts \-\-json /usr/local/go/bin/go | jq '.data.count'
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
A start address is where the linker says a function begins. It is not a boundary
derived from disassembly, and it is not the same as a symbol: a symbol can sit
mid\-function, and a function can have no symbol at all. Where the two disagree,
the symbol table is the one to distrust first.
.PP
The blob is a sequence of ULEB128 deltas from the image base, terminated by a
zero. A blob whose last value still has its continuation bit set is damaged or
was clipped; the partial value is dropped and
.B blobTruncated
is true, because a silently dropped value turns "the list ends here" into a
complete\-looking list that is one short.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR symlookup (1),
.BR sym (1),
.BR disasm (1),
.BR describe (1),
.BR nm (1)
